.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Wilhelmy (1850) followed the acid-catalyzed inversion of cane sugar by polarimetry: the optical rotation \alpha(t) drifts from the dextrorotatory sucrose value towards the (levorotatory) value of the glucose + fructose mixture. Because \alpha is linear in the concentrations, \alpha(t) - \alpha_\infty is proportional to the sucrose still present, and a straight line of \ln[(\alpha - \alpha_\infty)/(\alpha_0 - \alpha_\infty)] against t identifies -d[\text{sugar}]/dt = k[\text{sugar}].">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/kinetics/rate_laws/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_wilhelmy_sucrose_inversion_thumb.png
